 #: system/settings/settings.xml
-msgctxt "#41401"
-msgid "At 3840x2160 and 50 or 60 Hz, HDMI 2.0 does not carry RGB at 10 bits, so the graphics driver sends HDR as YCbCr 4:2:0 at 10 bits (or RGB at 8 bits). With this on, an HDR10, HLG or HDR10+ video goes out as YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits instead, with its HDR metadata as always: the display is told that it receives YCbCr 4:2:2, which HDMI carries at the speed of RGB at 8 bits, and Hades packs the picture in it the way it packs Dolby Vision. The colour of what Hades draws over the video (subtitles, the on-screen display) keeps twice the detail, and the video is carried exactly where the graphics card passes the pixels through unchanged. It is used only where the display says it takes 4:2:2, the driver can send it and nothing else has the output (Intel and AMD graphics), and not where \"Check the Dolby Vision output\" found that the card changes the pixels; anything it cannot take goes out as before, and the log says why. It applies with the next video. Off: HDR always goes out as before."
+msgctxt "#61461"
+msgid "Dolby Vision: apply the trims when adjusting to your display"
+msgstr ""
+
+#. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvtrimpass"; translated in Estuary (#53462)
+#: system/settings/settings.xml
+msgctxt "#61462"
+msgid "Some Dolby Vision films carry trims made for exactly your display's peak brightness. Where LibreELEC Hades adjusts Dolby Vision to your display itself (shown as HDR10 adjusted to the display, as SDR, or in Low-Latency), this decides whether it applies them. Off (the default): it does not, and the picture is adjusted from each scene's brightness alone. The standards describe only one way to apply them, as an example; compared with a studio's own version of a film for a 1000-nit display, that way made the shadows much darker than the studio did (in one scene, everything darker than about 11 nits turned black), so it stays off until the right way is known. On: they are applied that way. A TV that does Dolby Vision itself (native output) applies the trims on its own and is not affected by this. Nothing changes while \"Dolby Vision: ignore the studio's fine-tuning (trims)\" is on. Applies from the next video."
+msgstr ""
+
